{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A perturbation test in code execution is deterministic and universal; in human judgment, 'equivalent' perturbations are subjective and may introduce confounds.","Code either crashes or runs; human panels exhibit graded disagreement, making the threshold for 'detection' ambiguous."],"overturn_conditions":["The domain expert's intuition is demonstrably superior on items where the checklist is silent or misleading.","The non-expert panel cannot reliably perform the feature-detection step the checklist demands.","No checklist-equivalent perturbation can be constructed that is both valid and discriminating within a single evaluation."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a structured process (checklist/rule) intended to ensure reliable outcomes.","Both can suffer from a decoupling of process-adherence from genuine validity (mere compliance vs. cargo culting).","Both require a perturbation test to distinguish genuine understanding from procedural mimicry."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_002","SRC_005"],"transfer_claims":["The core transferable principle is that genuine reliability is sensitive to the *validity* of the inputs, not just their *format*. A perturbation that maintains the format but violates the validity exposes procedural mimicry.","The 'cargo cult' diagnostic applies directly: a panel that reaches the right answer for the wrong (procedural) reasons will fail when the procedural shortcut is exploited."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A trained non-expert panel using an explicit checklist will produce more reliable judgments on tasks with verifiable outcomes because the checklist externalizes the critical feature-detection step into an interpretable, auditable format, and aggregation averages out residual noise. Genuine reliability is distinguished from mere compliance by introducing targeted checklist-equivalent perturbations: substituting a superficially similar but decision-irrelevant feature for a genuine one. A panel showing genuine reliability will exhibit higher disagreement and error rates on perturbed items, whereas mere compliance will blindly follow the checklist to the same 'correct' answer, failing to register that the evidential basis has changed.","counterconditions":["The checklist is poorly designed and omits a critical feature that expert intuition reliably captures.","The task involves high-order feature interaction where the rules of combination are tacit and cannot be externalized into a linear checklist.","The perturbation is not truly equivalent in the eyes of the checklist, meaning it fails to isolate mere compliance from genuine semantic processing.","The non-expert panel is not truly independent, introducing correlated errors (e.g., shared training biases) that the aggregation cannot average out."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist transforms an ill-posed holistic judgment into a structured, multi-step algorithm. Non-experts act as feature detectors and rule appliers. Their independence ensures independent errors (e.g., lapses in attention) average out, while the checklist ensures the signal (critical features) is consistently extracted. The perturbation mechanism works because genuine reliability depends on the *validity* of the underlying features. If a panel's accuracy is merely procedural, it will treat a 'fake' feature that passes the checklist's formal criteria as a real one. If the reliability is genuine, the panel's underlying semantic processing will detect the anomaly, causing disagreement or an accuracy drop.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Deep learning models are trained on millions of examples; experts rely on far fewer, more varied experiences.","A checklist is an explicitly designed human artifact, while a neural network's architecture is an emergent property of optimization."],"overturn_conditions":["The non-expert panel, after training, develops a 'gestalt' sensitivity not captured by the explicit checklist.","The expert's intuition is no more reliable on edge cases than the panel's checklist-guided guesses."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a trade-off between a rigid, interpretable model (linear regression) and a flexible, opaque one (deep neural network/intuition).","Both can achieve high performance on standard cases but fail differently on out-of-distribution edge cases.","Both raise the question of whether a simpler model's success is 'genuine' understanding or mere pattern matching."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_004","SRC_006","SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["The 'right answer for the wrong reasons' problem applies to both: a checklist that works by summing features may be exploiting a spurious correlation in the evaluation set, just as a linear model might.","The failure mode on edge cases is a key diagnostic: rigid systems fail predictably, flexible ones unpredictably."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A single domain expert relying on intuition will produce more reliable judgments, because checklists necessarily fragment high-order feature integration into discrete, linear steps, losing critical gestalt information. Within a single evaluation, genuine reliability is distinguished from mere compliance by examining the panel's behavior on novel, 'checklist-silent' edge cases: a genuinely reliable process will exhibit coherent, principled extrapolation, while mere compliance will collapse into random guessing or systematic bias when the algorithm ends.","counterconditions":["The task is objectively verifiable and dominated by discrete, checkable features rather than holistic integration.","The expert's intuition is poorly calibrated due to lack of feedback or bias.","The 'checklist-silent' cases are not representative of the task's core difficulty.","The expert's 'principled extrapolation' is actually a post-hoc rationalization of a biased intuition."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is a model of complex, non-linear feature interactions honed by feedback. A checklist is a linear approximation of this model. On tasks where success depends on a simple sum of features, the checklist panel wins. On tasks where success depends on the specific configuration or interaction of features, the linear approximation fails and the expert wins. Genuine reliability is a property of the underlying generative model. A panel showing mere compliance lacks this model and will fail when the checklist's coverage ends.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["The Turing Test is open-ended and conversational; a checklist is closed and rigid.","A human in the Turing Test is actively trying to demonstrate understanding; a non-expert panel is trying to follow rules."],"overturn_conditions":["A checklist-equivalent perturbation test can be constructed that exposes the panel's lack of understanding, proving that outcome-accuracy alone is insufficient.","The expert achieves a higher accuracy than the panel, demonstrating the checklist's validity is incomplete."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a behaviorist approach to a mentalistic concept (understanding).","Both argue that if a system passes a sufficiently rigorous test, the internal mechanism is irrelevant.","Both face the challenge of constructing a test that is truly 'sufficiently rigorous' against deception."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_007"],"transfer_claims":["The core claim is that procedural success is sufficient evidence of competence, provided the procedure is valid.","The 'problem of other minds' applies: we can never truly know if the panel 'understands' or just 'complies', so we must judge by outcomes."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dichotomy between 'genuine reliability gain' and 'mere checklist compliance' is a false one within the scope of a single evaluation. If the panel achieves higher accuracy via strict adherence to a valid checklist, the compliance *is* the reliability gain. The only evidence needed is that the panel's accuracy is higher and the checklist's items are demonstrably causally linked to the objective outcome. The demand for a deeper distinction is an epistemological red herring.","counterconditions":["The checklist is valid only in the training distribution but fails in the evaluation distribution.","The objective outcome is causally linked to features the checklist does not capture, and the panel's success is due to a spurious correlation with checklist items.","The problem's framing explicitly demands a distinction between the two concepts, making this argument a rejection of the premise rather than an answer to it."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reliability is operationalized as consistency in achieving the correct outcome. If a checklist is a perfect algorithm for the task, compliance *guarantees* reliability. The 'genuineness' of the understanding is irrelevant to the practical outcome. Within a single evaluation, we can only measure outcomes. The distinction between 'real' and 'mere' implies a hidden variable (understanding) that cannot be measured, making the criteria unfulfillable by definition. We should instead focus on the *robustness* of the checklist's validity.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A map is a spatial representation; a checklist is a procedural one.","A map's validity is geographically fixed; a checklist's validity may depend on the user's execution."],"overturn_conditions":["The checklist incorporates mechanisms for handling uncertainty or novel cases, bridging the gap between proxy and model.","The non-expert panel's error patterns are demonstrably different from the checklist's theoretical failure modes, suggesting emergent understanding."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a proxy (map/checklist) for a reality (territory/expert knowledge).","Both can be perfectly useful and 'correct' within their domain of validity.","Both conflate the proxy with the reality at their peril."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_002"],"transfer_claims":["The 'proxy' transfer: a checklist that works is not a sign of genuine understanding, but of a valid proxy. The danger is when the proxy fails and the user has no deeper model to fall back on.","The underdetermination thesis: within a single evaluation (a single trip with the map), you cannot tell if the map is perfect or if you just got lucky."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A trained non-expert panel will not reliably outperform an expert because tacit knowledge, essential for many verifiable-outcome tasks, cannot be fully externalized into an explicit checklist. The distinction between genuine reliability and mere compliance is impossible to draw from a single evaluation because the panel's success on the evaluated items may be entirely due to the checklist having encoded the expert's tacit knowledge into a set of proxy rules that happen to work on that specific case-set. This is indistinguishable from genuine reliability without longitudinal data or counterfactual analysis.","counterconditions":["The task is sufficiently simple that tacit knowledge is minimal and the checklist is a lossless compression.","A single evaluation includes adversarial cases designed to break the proxy rules.","The panel's discussion transcripts reveal the formation of a causal model beyond the checklist."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument pivots on the 'tacit knowledge' problem (SRC_008). Expert intuition is often a compact encoding of deep causal models. A checklist is a lossy compression of this. On a single evaluation, the lossy compression may perform well if the test cases fall within the compressed regions. However, this is mere compliance with a proxy. Genuine reliability would require the panel to reconstruct the expert's causal model, which a checklist cannot do. A single evaluation cannot distinguish between a panel that has reconstructed the model and one that is merely following the proxy rules.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["In finance, returns are continuous and quantitative; in judgment, outcomes are often binary.","Financial models assume rational actors; human panel members are subject to social dynamics and shared biases."],"overturn_conditions":["The expert's intuition is demonstrably superior on items where the panel exhibits high disagreement.","The panel's disagreement is not predictive of error, suggesting it is not epistemically informative."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a portfolio of independent bets (judgments).","Both rely on the independence of errors to achieve a better expected outcome than a single bettor.","Both require that the variance of the individual bets be informative about the quality of the portfolio."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_005"],"transfer_claims":["The 'diversification benefit' is genuine only if the assets are truly independent. If the panel's errors are correlated (e.g., by a flawed checklist), the benefit is illusory.","The 'tracking error' is the diagnostic: if the portfolio (panel) tracks the benchmark (checklist) perfectly but fails to outperform in risk-adjusted terms (calibrated uncertainty), it's mere compliance."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's reliability gain is genuine if and only if the panel's collective judgment on checklist items demonstrates calibrated redundancy—specifically, if higher panel disagreement on a specific checklist item predicts a higher probability of an incorrect final answer, and this disagreement-accuracy correlation is stronger than the expert's self-reported confidence-accuracy correlation. This distinguishes genuine gain from mere compliance because 'mere compliance' implies mechanical adherence regardless of item difficulty, whereas genuine reliability implies the panel's uncertainty is epistemically informative.","counterconditions":["The checklist forces a binary choice on each item, eliminating the possibility of calibrated redundancy.","The expert's intuition is already well-calibrated, making the panel's redundancy signal no more informative.","Panel disagreement is driven by personality clashes rather than task difficulty."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Genuine reliability is not just about getting the right answer; it's about *knowing when you don't know*. A panel that merely complies with a checklist will produce answers with uniformly high confidence (because the checklist is followed). A panel that has genuinely internalized the task's logic will exhibit calibrated uncertainty: members will disagree on hard items, and this disagreement will be predictive of errors. If this internal signal of difficulty is present and more reliable than the expert's gut feeling, the reliability gain is genuine, not merely procedural.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A philosophical interlocutor can adapt questions in real-time; a single evaluation requires a fixed set of probes.","Socratic dialogue assumes the knowledge is present but latent; the panel may genuinely lack the knowledge."],"overturn_conditions":["The panel produces justifications that are causally valid but use a different vocabulary than the expert, making automated comparison fail.","The expert's justifications are no better than the panel's, suggesting the expert also relies on mere pattern recognition."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a confrontational dialogue designed to expose the limits of a system's knowledge.","Both distinguish between a system that 'has the right answer' and a system that 'knows why the answer is right'.","Both treat the inability to extrapolate beyond a fixed rule set as a sign of mere mimicry."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["The 'aporia' transfer: if the panel is reduced to silence or contradiction when questioned beyond the checklist, it demonstrates that the checklist was a crutch, not a scaffold for understanding.","The distinction between 'true opinion' and 'knowledge' is the core target: the panel may have true opinion (compliance) but lacks knowledge (genuine reliability)."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere checklist compliance by forcing the panel and the expert to justify their judgments on a subset of *novel* cases not covered by the checklist, using a common vocabulary. If the panel's justifications exhibit the same causal reasoning patterns and predictive power as the expert's, the gain is genuine. If the panel's justifications are merely a restatement of the checklist rules or appeal to authority, it is mere compliance.","counterconditions":["The domain is one where verbalization destroys the intuition (e.g., face recognition).","The panel is able to construct plausible-sounding but incorrect rationalizations (confabulation).","The expert is unable to articulate their own intuition, making the comparison impossible."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"This approach directly probes the underlying mental model rather than inferring it from outcomes. Mere compliance implies a 'black box' adherence to rules. Genuine reliability implies a 'glass box' understanding of the causal model. By forcing verbalization on novel cases, we test whether the panel has acquired the expert's generative model or merely a discriminative rule set. The common vocabulary ensures we are comparing like with like.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2}],"simulation_proposals":[]}